                    {
                        "sense_id": "6338cc0f-400a-36b3-bdce-76438deb5f49",
                        "entry_id": "2e3b234f-b4e1-3a2b-a929-cc39202244df",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Principes du capitalisme appliqués au domaine caritatif."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "neologism"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "1fe13126-6ada-4f2d-b53b-043e3432906d",
                                "sense_id": "6338cc0f-400a-36b3-bdce-76438deb5f49",
                                "text": "Bill Gates, Warren Buffet. A eux deux, ils ont mis à disposition de la fondation Melinda Gates, près de 60 milliards de dollars. Une somme rondelette. A l’instar de ces deux hommes d’affaires, d’autres personnalités mettent leur fortune et leurs carnets d’adresses au service des autres. Surnommé «philanthrocapitalisme», ce mouvement a fait des émules."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "874fa83a-758b-4a83-92c2-0127706a541d",
                                "sense_id": "6338cc0f-400a-36b3-bdce-76438deb5f49",
                                "text": "Diffuser dans les agricultures africaines traditionnelles, parfois biologiques, les herbicides et autres produits de synthèse génèrerait d’énormes profits, conformément à la philosophie de ces fondations « caritatives » un peu particulières qui prétendent mêler diffusion du progrès technique et récolte de dividendes. Une nouvelle forme de « charity business » qui a même un nom : la revue anglaise The Economist l’a récemment baptisée « philanthrocapitalisme »."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "2d9746d2-55e4-41cb-8f89-9b3c7f0066c5",
                        "source_entry_id": "2e3b234f-b4e1-3a2b-a929-cc39202244df",
                        "source_sense_id": "6338cc0f-400a-36b3-bdce-76438deb5f49",
                        "target_lang_word": "philanthrocapitalism",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "philanthrocapitalism",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "8b898e9f-e7e9-3561-bc8b-d23dff528514",
                                        "entry_id": "41a3f703-e60b-3c19-a46e-38996769f97f",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The philosophy of applying capitalist style objectives and criteria to directing philanthropic enterprises."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "neologism",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ee55f0f6-f933-4b14-9af7-da724bf84370",
                                                "sense_id": "8b898e9f-e7e9-3561-bc8b-d23dff528514",
                                                "text": "At its best, philanthrocapitalism reinforces and amplifies the time, money, skills, and gifts given every year by people who are not rich, and it informs and enhances government policies."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4acd60ec-b2c1-49f0-901d-a139aa0e69fd",
                                                "sense_id": "8b898e9f-e7e9-3561-bc8b-d23dff528514",
                                                "text": "\"Philanthrocapitalism\", as it has been called, veers towards tackling symptoms of poverty and distress rather than underlying causes."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cd488eab-83e9-40b2-9f58-cb2d1fa6ed47",
                                                "sense_id": "8b898e9f-e7e9-3561-bc8b-d23dff528514",
                                                "text": "‘Philanthrocapitalism’—when donors apply the business techniques they honed in their professional lives to charitable giving—has made a major impact on how the rich choose to give money, and to what causes. […] But the problem is that philanthrocapitalism is an uneasy fit with culture, which has always struggled to prove its worth in a way that can be quantified."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
